# Counting Words with Elixir

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
  {:req, "~> 0.5"}
])
```

## Introduction

This notebook counts the most common words in a short text, first with plain
`Enum` functions and then with a stream, so it scales to files that do not fit
in memory.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

The pipeline has three steps:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A[Text] --> B[Split into words]
  B --> C[Count]
  C --> D[Top 10]
```

## Plain Enum

```elixir
text = """
Alice was beginning to get very tired of sitting by her sister on the bank,
and of having nothing to do: once or twice she had peeped into the book her
sister was reading, but it had no pictures or conversations in it.
"""

text
|> String.downcase()
|> String.split(~r/[^\p{L}']+/u, trim: true)
|> Enum.frequencies()
|> Enum.sort_by(fn {_word, count} -> -count end)
|> Enum.take(5)
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[{"her", 2}, {"of", 2}, {"sister", 2}, {"the", 2}, {"to", 2}]
```

## Streaming a large file

```elixir
"alice.txt"
|> File.stream!()
|> Stream.flat_map(&String.split(String.downcase(&1), ~r/[^\p{L}']+/u, trim: true))
|> Enum.reduce(%{}, fn word, acc -> Map.update(acc, word, 1, &(&1 + 1)) end)
|> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 1), :desc)
|> Enum.take(10)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "Top words")
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```text
%Kino.DataTable{name: "Top words", rows: 10}
```

> **Tip:** `File.stream!/1` reads line by line, so memory stays flat no matter
> how long the book is.

